WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.930
GAUTAMI NADKARNI: Hey, Lorin.

00:00:00.930 --> 00:00:01.500
Let's go.

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:03.500
I thought we were going
to go out for ice cream.

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:04.860
LORIN PRICE: Oh, sorry.

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:07.080
I'm trying to cleanse
my data, and it's taking

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:08.370
so much longer than I thought.

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:11.010
And I'm trying to learn Python,
and it's just not going well.

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:12.600
GAUTAMI NADKARNI: Well, why?

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:15.220
Google has a managed service
for this called Dataprep.

00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:15.900
Let me show you.

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:19.260
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:25.710
Let's get right into
the Dataprep tool.

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:28.130
If you're using the
Dataprep for the first time,

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:32.340
accept approvals and give it
couple of seconds to load.

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:34.560
We can start by creating a flow.

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:37.830
Give it a name and
description, and click Create.

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:39.930
You have the option
of importing data

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:42.060
either from your local
machine, Google Cloud

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:43.860
Storage, or BigQuery.

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:47.640
For this demo, we are going
to use Cloud Storage bucket.

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:50.460
I have preloaded a few
ice cream sales data sets

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:51.780
into Cloud Storage.

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:55.440
This is the data set I will
use for the Dataprep flow.

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:57.990
Click on the data set you want
to begin the transformation

00:00:57.990 --> 00:01:00.860
with and click on Edit Recipe.

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:03.210
A recipe contains
a sequence of steps

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:06.750
that transforms the selected
data set into a new data set.

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:09.300
The Wrangle language is
used to create recipes.

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:10.500
LORIN PRICE: Uh-oh.

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:11.520
Wrangle language?

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:13.350
Am I still going to have to
learn another programming

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:13.890
language?

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:15.330
GAUTAMI NADKARNI: Actually, no.

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:17.460
Wrangle language is
a lot like formulas

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:19.110
you see in Google Sheets.

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:21.220
And you don't have to
write it on your own.

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:23.040
A lot of it is
auto-generated for you.

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:23.790
LORIN PRICE: Phew.

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:24.630
OK, good.

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:25.725
Let's keep going.

00:01:25.725 --> 00:01:27.210
GAUTAMI NADKARNI:
For this demo, we

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:29.550
will build tasks
performed by data analysts

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:33.150
regularly like cleansing,
structuring, and enriching.

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:35.310
As you can see, this
is the basic view

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:38.640
of how data looks across
different columns and rows.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:42.210
After looking at data, you
might see some mismatched values

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:44.550
in red for one of the columns.

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:46.980
Upon clicking it, you might
realize that something

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:48.460
was wrong with the data--

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:51.510
in this case, the at
symbol in the number field.

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:54.000
The tool also gives you
some auto suggestions

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:56.320
for this column to choose from.

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:59.430
I'm going to fix this column
by using a value given to me

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:02.100
by the business analyst for
a typical serial number.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:07.170
Split by delimiter
allows you to separate

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:10.454
values in the same column by
a delimiter-- in this case,

00:02:10.454 --> 00:02:11.204
the forward slash.

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:16.630
I'm going to rename
these columns now

00:02:16.630 --> 00:02:18.970
for better understanding.

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:23.480
We can also easily join multiple
data sets in just a few clicks.

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:25.750
Let's join two
data sets with one

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:29.580
of the existing data set that
we have loaded in the flow.

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:32.800
Type ID is automatically
detected as the join key,

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:34.600
and you can choose
which column you

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:36.170
want to display
for both the data

00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:38.620
sets post the join function.

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:41.830
To hide a column, simply
right click on the column

00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:43.180
and hide it.

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:45.580
Similarly, I'm
going to be joining

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:50.380
one more data set using flavor
ID to further enrich my data.

00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:54.190
Changing data types is
super simple as well.

00:02:54.190 --> 00:02:57.100
Let's remove the percentage
symbol from the profit margin

00:02:57.100 --> 00:03:01.600
percentage column and convert
it into [? integer ?] data type.

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:04.240
Lastly, let's apply
some custom logic

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:06.850
to create a new data column.

00:03:06.850 --> 00:03:08.890
The CASE function
allows you to perform

00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:11.800
multiple conditional tests
on a set of expressions

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:13.630
with a statement.

00:03:13.630 --> 00:03:17.110
Here, I'm going to define a
range of profits and demands

00:03:17.110 --> 00:03:20.800
from low, medium, and high based
on the ranges of the value they

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:22.180
might fall in.

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:26.050
After these steps, my
pipeline is now ready to run.

00:03:26.050 --> 00:03:28.420
I'll select a couple
options for how

00:03:28.420 --> 00:03:30.490
I want my results to be stored.

00:03:30.490 --> 00:03:34.270
I can also choose if I want
the job to run manually

00:03:34.270 --> 00:03:35.170
or scheduled.

00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:37.325
And voila, we can run the job.

00:03:37.325 --> 00:03:40.360
LORIN PRICE: Wow, this
is really cool, and easy.

00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:43.740
Once I create the recipes,
I can run the jobs anytime.

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:45.400
And I don't even
need to learn Python.

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:46.750
GAUTAMI NADKARNI: Absolutely.

00:03:46.750 --> 00:03:49.918
Can we now eat our ice creams?

00:03:49.918 --> 00:03:52.420
LORIN PRICE: Don't forget to
subscribe for more great Google

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:53.537
Cloud Platform content.

00:03:53.537 --> 00:03:55.120
GAUTAMI NADKARNI:
Thanks for watching.

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:58.470
[MUSIC PLAYING]

